cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or refer to the paper, please cite it as below."
title: "Predicting the Goal-Plane Entry of a Penalty Kick from Two Early Ball Positions"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "UPC 2026 Team 230"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ziyepei06/upc-2026-problem-b"
license: MIT
year: 2026
version: "9.0"
date-released: "2026-11-08"
abstract: >-
  UPC 2026 Problem B. Two measured ball positions at t = 0.05 s and t = 0.10 s
  are used to fit a six-parameter transverse-force model; the goal-plane entry
  point (2.785, 1.945) m, its calibrated intervals, the model and scenario
  envelopes, and an observation-design study are reported. The paper also
  measures the drag coefficient on real 2-D tracking data and shows that the
  three given coordinates imply a deceleration above the 95th percentile of real
  trajectories.
keywords:
  - penalty kick
  - goal-plane prediction
  - Magnus force
  - inverse problem
  - Bayesian uncertainty quantification
  - identifiability
